              "text": "**Yes, historically, Coca-Cola did contain cocaine.** \n\nWhen the beverage was first created in the late 19th century, cocaine was a legal and active ingredient. \n\nThe history of how cocaine got into Coca-Cola, how much was actually in it, and how it was eventually removed involves several key stages:\n\n### 1. Why was it in there?\nCoca-Cola was invented in 1885 by John Stith Pemberton, an Atlanta pharmacist who had become addicted to morphine after being wounded in the Civil War. He originally created a product called \"Pemberton\u2019s French Wine Coca,\" which combined wine, cocaine, and kola nut extract. \n\nWhen Atlanta passed temperance laws in 1885, Pemberton removed the alcohol and created a sweet, carbonated syrup. He named it **Coca-Cola** after its two primary active ingredients:\n*   **\"Coca\"** from the South American *coca leaf*, which contained active cocaine.\n*   **\"Cola\"** from the West African *kola nut*, which provided caffeine.\n\nAt the time, cocaine was widely believed to be a safe, miracle medicine. Coca-Cola was marketed as a \"brain tonic\" and patent medicine that could cure headaches, fatigue, and nervous exhaustion.\n\n### 2. How much cocaine was in a glass?\nWhile the exact original recipe remains a closely guarded secret, historians estimate that in the late 1880s, a single glass of Coca-Cola contained roughly **9 milligrams of cocaine**. \n\nFor comparison, a modern recreational \"line\" of snorted cocaine is typically around 50 to 75 milligrams. While 9 milligrams was not enough to cause a massive, immediate high, it was certainly enough to give drinkers a noticeable, euphoric \"kick\" and mild stimulation.\n\n### 3. When and why was it removed?\nBy the late 1890s and early 1900s, public and medical opinions on cocaine shifted drastically. It became clear that cocaine was highly addictive and dangerous. Additionally, racial anxieties in the American South\u2014specifically media-fueled fears that cocaine use among Black laborers was leading to crime\u2014prompted the company to act. \n\n*   **1903:** Under the leadership of Asa Candler, Coca-Cola began transitioning away from active cocaine. They stopped using fresh coca leaves and switched to \"spent\" or decocainized coca leaves (leaves that had already had the cocaine alkaloid chemically extracted). \n*   **1929:** The extraction process was perfected to ensure that the coca leaf flavoring extract used in the soda was **100% cocaine-free**. \n\n### 4. Is there any connection to cocaine today?\nStrangely, yes. **Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves to flavor its soda today**. \n\nBecause the federal government tightly regulates coca leaves, only one company in the United States is legally allowed to import and process them: the **Stepan Company** in Maywood, New Jersey. \n\nUnder strict DEA supervision, the Stepan Company imports about 100 metric tons of coca leaves each year from South America. They extract the cocaine from the leaves, create a decocainized syrup, and sell this flavoring agent exclusively to The Coca-Cola Company. The pure cocaine they extract doesn't go to waste; it is sold to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a legal topical anesthetic (cocaine hydrochloride) used by doctors for nose, throat, and eye surgeries.",
